      "math_academy_id": "q-98170",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{5}{2}⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩+4⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{5}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩+⟨3,\\,2,\\,-10⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}+1,\\,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
